e impact. So it's kind of those, you know, you don't I don't recommend tipping over a $4,000 spotter onto a concrete floor. But that's sort of that that idiot test. Right. That you got to do because it it will happen despite your best efforts, especially for the customer for that. They're taking it into battle. I was just going to say if you're calling it an idiot test, I'm not going to confess to the times I've done just that. Yeah, I've dropped a few things. I've certainly taken a few tumbles. Right. You spend enough time on on a mountainside with scree fields. And, yeah, I don't believe anybody that says that they've stayed on their feet 100 percent of the time. Yeah. Do you know if there are any other scope manufacturers in the U.S. still that build assemble 100 percent in the U.S.? Yeah, there's there's nothing that compares to what Leupold and Stevens does. There's a lot of people that that now wish they could and would love to bring things back. And and the unfortunate thing is that all that knowledge is is gone. And it's so hard to build, you know, to build over 100 years of optics knowledge like Leupold and Stevens has. There's nothing like time. Right. So you could start now and in 100 years you might have get to the level that we're kind of at. But, you know, there'll be people that generally if they're marking their product, you know, maybe assembled in the U.S. There's certain legal terms. So a lot of people kind of confuse that on the bottom. But, you know, design machined assembled. What we're doing here from owning all of the design to the basically all of the metal turning and milling is on our machines here in house. We own the anodizing company when we send it out and it goes down the road. We own that company. So we're we spec all of our anodizing again to mill standards all the way to then assembling the full optic from the raw components. I'd say that the closest thing anybody does that's even in the realm of assembling something is they're buying, you know, full eyepieces, you know, erector systems, maybe an objective kind of package. And they're doing kind of that fat, that last piece of kind of assembly. They didn't machine any parts, but they're doing some some assembly and they're they're collimating and they're they're making sure that that that final piece is in alignment. But you're you know, you're 80 to 90 percent done with the optic by that point. Yeah, especially if you if you account for all the design and testing and all that stuff that comes prior to that and all that integration, you know, you're doing the last couple percent. Yeah. And you wouldn't have control over tolerances and anything like that. Absolutely. Yeah. So even if you wanted to improve it in those cases, you can't because you can't necessarily make a slightly different skew. Right. Because it's it's going into a couple of different brands products. And that manufacturer doesn't want to change the one part just for you unless unless you can move, you know, 100000 scopes or something. They might make you one off, but you can't just be like, hey, can you put a little bit more, you know, Loctite there? Can you change these threads to make sure they're a little bit more accurate or, hey, I want to do a little bit better tracking. They just say, like, no. Right. It's you can't change one thing just for you. So, yep. It you know, we always see our competition come and go. There's new brands that come into existence and go out of existence every year. Right. And generally sourcing a lot of the same stuff from from those different areas. You know, our competition is not just on a brand side, but it really is those other optics makers that are in China, you know, some in Japan or Philippines or things. That's what it was a U.S. manufacturer, American made. That's who we're competing against. Right. And so they definitely have us on their sites. They're doing the same thing. I'm sure the VX4 came out there. They're buying those. They're tearing them down and they're trying to understand how they reverse engineer this and reverse engineer it. Right. So you always got to stay ahead of them. But that's it's a great privilege to be able to do that. That's why I got into this. I'd studied manufacturing engineering and just loved engineering getting into it. And I just have always had a passion for building things here. Always been a pretty hands on type of person fixing stuff and kind of doing things. And so being able to find a company that is like, hey, I'm not just inventing something. I'm passing it off to somebody else to make. Like, you know, they have a hand in each part of it and then be able to use the product at the end of the day. I haven't been able to find anything else that's like that in the U.S. to do engineering on. Like, not just rifle scopes or our industry, but just period. Right. This this checks all those boxes for me personally. No, it's pretty cool. Just to clarify, are all of your scopes made in the U.S. or the freedom line made overseas? So, yeah, we have a motto all the way from our CEO. We call it the moment of truth. So if there is a trigger being pulled, so any optic you mount on a firearm that has a trigger, it comes out of this factory. Very cool. And so that's every single red dot optic. Right. That's every single scope line. And it is designed, machined, assembled in this factory. We have a vast, you know, product line all the way from, you know, outerwear that I'm wearing here to hats and other things that we source. And we spend a lot of time with our partners there. We actually have from the binocular lines and other things that we source, we will pay for actually develop the tooling and the manufacturing techniques and then bring it and help set it up in their factories. And we have little loophole factories walled off in their factories. So we kind of go to that level when we source things. But that's usually on, you know, some of our spotters and some of our binos. And then we make a lot of our spotters in-house here as well. So the Mark IV spotter has been one over 30 years that we built in this factory. And then again, I mentioned just today that MX-6 that launched that's on our website is made right here in this factory as well. So it extends past kind of the shooting optics. But that is that one thing that, you know, every customer can think of or guarantee is when that moment of truth, you can't ever take that bullet back once you pull that trigger. And so we want to make sure that we own that and fully own that from start to finish. So at that moment of truth, you're looking through an optic that came out of this factory. Hello, backcountry hunters. This is John Zombrough from the Lifetime Athlete. And this edition of Backcountry Fitness and Nutrition is brought to you by Spartan Precision Equipment. Hunting season is kicking off in fine fashion and it's about time for another challenge. Today, I've got one for you, which is popular in the Lifetime Athlete app, and it's the sleep challenge. This is indeed a tricky one because during the season, we struggle with all those early morning risings that we typically encounter. But that stated adaptation as well as recovery occurs primarily when we sleep. So your challenge is to give your body the most important opportunity it needs to be optimized in health, fitness, and performance. Here's your sleep challenge. Every night this week, shoot for a minimum of 7.5 hours of sleep per night. That's roughly five sleep cycles. Each lasts about 75 to 90 minutes. If you can get nine hours, which is six cycles, that's even better. We're putting the effort into sleeping for these recovery purposes. Try to get into bed at the same time every night, including the weekend. Let's also use a 4-3-2-1 countdown as follows. Four hours before bed, no more training. Three hours before bed, no more food. Two hours before bed, no fluorescent or LED lights. You can use incandescents or halogens only. And one hour before bed, no more screen time. This is the tough one for modern humans. I think we'd all admit that. Do these things and you'll stay fresh and focused all season long. This segment was provided courtesy of Spartan Precision Equipment, makers of the most innovative and ultralight bipods, tripods, and accessories for the backcountry hunter. Legendary Spartan toughness. Purpose built and made to last a lifetime. Learn more at javelinbipod.com. It's been a lot of fun learning about some of these, you know, backseat, backstage. I'm mixing my metaphor. Just a look behind the curtain with Leupold there. Is there anything else you'd like our listeners to know before we wrap up here? I think the biggest thing is that there's, you know, 750 Americans sitting in this building that are there to serve you, whether it's answering a question or it's making sure that your product is right day in and day out for generations to come. So, you know, if you ever have a question or a comment or anything, there's a person right down the hall that picks up the call, right, or responds to your emails. And a lot of those get forwarded to the engineering department and things as well. I used to get calls. They mixed up my phone line one time and I was getting calls on my desk phone, which was actually great, right? Like, I wanted to keep some of that going because it was cool to talk with folks. But that's pretty unique, I think, is that you got a company right here in Beaverton, Oregon, that's dedicated to doing this day in and day out. And we have a fantastic team that is full of hunters and shooters themselves. Not everybody, but a lot of people here have this as a passion. So we love to hear from our customers about ways that we can make our products better, improve, or help them kind of get set up with their lifetime hunt, whether that's, you know, in the back 40 over a cornfield for hunting for a whitetail, or they got a once-in-a-lifetime trip out, you know, in Alaska somewhere for moose or anywhere in between. So, yeah, we're here for you. Outstanding. Love to hear it. You know, I've, by nature of my job, because, you know, I got into this as a writer. I was an editor for six years in-house, editor-in-chief of Shooting Times for four of those years, and then went to writing full-time 15 years ago. And as part of my job, you know, it's a responsibility to use all kinds of different optics. And as a result, I've used and liked a lot of different scopes and scope brands. But when it came time to partnering with somebody on the podcast here, which is my baby, right? That belongs to me. It's not a magazine I write for. And I was able to partner with Leupold. That just made my day, my year, my decade, because I'd go into the safe and personal rifles, I'd pull them out. They've got a gold ring on them. And when I realized that, you know, I had this moment. It shouldn't have been an epiphany, but it kind of was. I looked in there and I thought, clearly, this is what I trust. It's what I favor naturally when I'm hunting without an assignment. You know, I'm taking a personal rifle. I'm going out with a scope that I trust. They're American-made. It's a great brand. I've got good friends at Leupold. And so I guess that's just a long-winded way of saying I consider it a real privilege and honor to be partnered with Leupold on the podcast. And to you personally, Dustin, just thank you for coming on and sharing some, you know, engineering insight and wisdom with our listeners. I really appreciate it. Yeah, you're very welcome. And I feel the same way. Like, I had a safe full of a couple gold rings and I wouldn't work in another scope company. I mean, it'd just be hard, right, to not be that authentic, right? Is, hey, this is the best one. You see that gold ring on it out there in those hunting applications. And being able to work and kind of be a part of it, right, of this tradition that's over 100 years old is pretty awesome. Yeah, it's easy to ride for the brand when you love the brand. That's right. Folks, thanks for tuning in. Hit me with questions about, well, anything hunting, backcountry, optical. You know, if I can't answer them for you, I know where smart people like Dustin are. And I can probably find an answer to those questions. Just email us at backcountryhuntingpodcast at gmail.com. Of course, check out our social media sites and follow along on patreon.com slash backcountry for our exclusive bonus content for our Patreon members there. Dustin, thanks once again for joining us. It's been a real privilege. Joseph von Benedict with the Backcountry Hunting Podcast. And we'll see you out there.
