## Item text
Hi and welcome to the Long Range Shooting and Custom Rifle Building Podcast presented by Wolf Precision Incorporated, where we learn about and share long range shooting and custom rifle building. I am your host, Jamie Dotson, and welcome to the show. Hi and welcome to episode 286 of Wolf Precision's Long Range Shooting and Custom Rifle Building Podcast. I am your host, Jamie Dotson, and welcome back to the show. In this episode, we're going to be talking about the wind and mastering the wind and giving you some food for thought as well as some training techniques and really the concept of learn to go slow to go fast. So without further ado, here we go. Before I get started, I want to say a quick thank you to MDT. They are a maker of a lot of great stocks and chassis, a lot of great accessories. A really great way to upgrade your rifle, custom or factory, is with an aftermarket stock that fits you properly that has full adjustability, adjustable length of pull and cheek piece. So if you want to upgrade your rifle, you can stop over and look at the fine selection at mdttac.com. That's mdttac.com. I'd also like to thank Krieger Barrels. Krieger is the maker of fine-cut rifle barrels, one of the finest barrel makers in the country, one of the oldest Benchrist barrel makers in the country. They are a family-owned and operated business out of the great state of Wisconsin. We use them for almost all of our builds here, with the exception of a few other barrels that are under customer requests. So if you want the best and you need it now, they offer a new program, Krieger Direct. We can log in and have your barrel in as little as two to three business days from in-stock inventory. So stop over and look at all the selection that Krieger has at kriegerbarrels.com. That's kriegerbarrels.com today. So we had a customer and good friend, someone that helps us a lot in the machining world, really knowledgeable on machines and machining as well, really get to spend a lot of time with them. We have built him a couple custom rifles and he is currently competing. We were speaking about some projects that we're working on yesterday, and one of the things that he's struggling with is the wind. So he's using a caliber right now, I think the 22 Creed in competition. And besides the timing thing, which we'll talk about that in the next podcast, he's really struggling with getting the wind calls down. And I think it's starting to take a little bit of a toll on him. Of course, everybody wants to do better and you want to see improvements. And so unfortunately, if he's doing like most people do, the first thing that they're going to step into is they're going to try to beat the winded submission with horsepower. And that's exactly what we talked about yesterday. So Joe, if you're listening, I've been thinking about this all night and it's just on my mind this morning. So I figured it was a great opportunity not only to share with, you know, you, the thinking that I would have behind it and what I would say long-term be more beneficial, as well as to share with customers and listeners out there that are going to go through the same thing. So his first response is to go bigger in caliber. Now, it is true that a lot of people are going back away from the 6mms and 22 calibers, and you'll see a lot more 6.5s coming onto the line. And the reason being is just they've got super high BCs, even though they're not the fastest kid on the block, their BCs are absolutely incredible. And they have a little bit of an advantage in the wind when you get that BC added to that. And so you're going to go up in caliber, you're going to go up in recoil, you're going to go up in all those areas to try to get that BC to beat them when in submission. And for match shooting, maybe. But what I would suggest here, and my advice after thinking about this all night, like how would I approach this by someone sitting across the kitchen table and me having an open, honest conversation with them? Here's what I would say. Learn to go slow before you go fast. In order to master the wind, you have to learn to do it with skill. You cannot be in a submission with horsepower. What little gains you will get off the BC does not compensate your inabilities to read it correctly or to have the confidence to read it correctly. I'm going to share with you a couple of things that I think are relevant in areas where the same rule applies and the amount of skills that are taken away from it. So, for example, let's talk about sailing for a moment. Most real sailors will tell you, get a dinghy, a sailing dinghy, or something similar, and go out and learn to sail. You are going to get your butt kicked. You're probably going to get flipped over. You're probably going to make a lot of mistakes. But because those dinghies are so sensitive or there's really small racing boats that you'll see people sailing with, I think a sunfish is another one you'll see that's common, is they are super sensitive to your inputs and correct inputs, and they are quick to punish you for bad inputs. So, when you go and you get a 32 or 36-foot sailboat that weighs 12,000 to 15,000 pounds or more, seven tons, massive sails, and a lot of power. Well, when those winds pick up, yeah, the ship's going to be more stable, but the odds of getting severely hurt or getting out of control or getting into a situation where you can't control it anymore are high, and so are the consequences. So, you really should cut your teeth on something that's much smaller and more sensitive to learn to be a sailor first before you start getting into these rigs that, quote-unquote, can handle the big storm. You know, you'll hear this all the time. There's a lot of legendary sailboats out there that are, quote-unquote, blue water, safe, get in scary conditions with the weather turns bad and all these things happen. You know, you want a boat that can take care of you, and I get that part. But you should also learn to control it and have confidence in your ability to maintain that boat, maintain the sails, direction, control the boat, don't lose control of the boat. And that is learned through a dinghy. And from everyone that I've talked to, they go from that, they get into racing dinghies, and then they get into, like, racing sailboats. There's small ones, of course, not, you know, I'm talking like 60-foot boats, but, I mean, I'm sure there's people that get up there and race those size of boats, but, you know, let's just say for the average guy out there, probably not into cars right away, but start with the dinghy. Another one that I can personally relate to is learning the art of riding a motorcycle. There are a lot of motorcycle riders out there, and there are some that like to ride in the dirt. And myself, I'm 54, soon to be 55. I grew up on dirt bikes. I love riding in the dirt. I love the challenge of it. And I had a friend of mine introduce me to a sport called trials riding and learning to ride a trials bike. And the same thing applies here that applies to sailing. Learn to go slow to go fast. Master the art of balance, throttle, and clutch control in super slow, tight technical conditions, and it will enhance your ability to ride a dirt bike or, you know, any type of a Doro type bike or street bike for that matter. Way better. Way, way better. Now, insanely better. As a matter of fact, your hard Enduro races are some of the hardest races in the world when it comes to motorcycles. Almost all of them are repeatedly being won by people who came from trials bikes backgrounds because they have that really fine finesse and control over the motorcycle. At slow speed, with confidence, they can get the bike to do all kinds of things that most people can't dream of doing. So, for example, he had convinced me to buy a trials bike. Like, I really want to learn the art. I get sucked into things and I like to learn the art of stuff. It's just part of my nature, right? So, I have a trials bike. And I called him after a couple weeks of it and I was like, oh my God, this is the hardest thing I think I've ever done on a motorcycle. And A, it's extremely physical. I mean, you would think going slow and doing all these static balance things that, you know, anyone can do it. But I will tell you, 5, 10, 15 minutes of doing it, it is an absolute workout. It's insane and it's hard. But I will tell you, I see an improvement with my riding on my street bike all the time. I mean, I can see it. And I can see, like we have dirt bikes as well. I can see my improvement, my confidence, all of that happening because I'm learning these ultra hard, slow maneuvers, how to really finesse the bike to get it to do what you want it to do. For example, one of the things you should do on a trials bike is you stand still. Feet on the pegs, balanced. No movement, right? You're not going forward. You're stopping and actually maintaining your balance on the bike, standing upright. Now you can turn and go left or right with the front wheel. That helps a little bit to give you something else to sort of rock against. But try to do that for two or three minutes of just standing perfectly still on the foot pegs on the motorcycle without falling over. It's way harder than you think. I mean, it kicks my butt. And I am learning so much, but the same rules apply. Like if you want to become a great enduro rider, you want to feel confident when that big log is coming up. Well, what most people do is they just hammer on the throttle and they hit it harder. They see a big scary hill and they pick a line and they just roll the throttle on and away they go, right? But if you have this really awesome control, the clutch, the motorcycle, the throttle, you can pick your line and you don't have to go 150 mile an hour. When in doubt, throttle out. That's sort of like one of the craziest things you get people hurt all the time. You can pick your line, you can be safe, you can control the bike up through it and you don't feel out of control. And you've got this finesse control that's crazy. So one more, there's a really great hard enduro rider out there that's won many massive events. And some of these guys, if you get a chance to watch, some of it are held in Europe that are just absolutely, brutally hard. I mean, most people don't, don't even finish half the race. It's that hard. But one of the things when they asked him after the interview and he was a, he was a trials rider is he said that everybody was picking these lines that looked like that was the line you're supposed to pick. He was able to pick these much harder areas to ride through and he did it with ease because of all the things he learned that the motorcycle can do at slow speed. And so he would just go out around everybody in an area that you think would be impossible to ride a motorcycle and up over he goes and around the corner and off he goes. It's amazing. The same thing goes with reading the wind. So I'm going to circle back and talk about the wind here for a moment. Rather than going up in caliber and trying to beat it into submission with horsepower or BC, I'm really going to suggest that you go down in a .223 bolt gun or a .22 arc and take it out and learn the art of the wind. You're going to be more exposed to mistakes. The mistakes are going to punish you quickly just like a trials bike or just like a sailing dinghy. You screw up a little bit and away the shot goes. Way off plate. Right? But you will learn and grow more as a shooter when it comes to reading the wind and then when you get the bigger calibers .22 Creed, 6.5 Creed, 6.5 by 47 Lapua, 6 Dasher, whatever it might be. You now have the skill set. You've got complete control of the rifle and your abilities to read the wind better and have confidence in your abilities because you've done it with calibers that are harder and now you can take full advantage of the easier calibers. I remember years ago, Mark Machensky owns MKM Precision north of Pittsburgh. Mark used to help me a lot here with the shooting schools. Mark is a great shooter. We've built him several rifles over the years. I remember he went down to Virginia somewhere and I convinced Mark to buy .223 for a trainer. Really enjoyed having him as one of my helpers here at the school and now they have the MKM Precision and they hold matches north of Pittsburgh. Really doing a great job. Can't wait to go up and shoot a match with them and see them again. It's been a while. But I'm going to bring up the story because it's really relevant. Mark had signed up for this class that he was going to take for this field shooting and one of our other guys, Walt, had signed up as well and they called and said, hey, we're going to get down and shoot for the day at this place. You want to go along? And I was like, I got the day off. Don't have any plans around the house. Life is busy. I grabbed my rifle and I went and we all took two 23s. What was amazing is when we got on to the actual shooting portion in the afternoon, it was like a field shooting positional class that he was holding. I think Mark wanted to get down and learn some things from him. So yeah, sure. He learned something new from people all the time and we're probably talking something like 15, 16, 17 years ago, right? Well, when we got out onto the actual range, it was windy and it was a big farm, really just farm with targets all up over the place. And the guy was like, what calibers did you guys bring? And we're like, oh, we brought our trainers two, two threes. And he sort of got off that. It's like, really? You brought that here to shoot? And I mean, it's not uncommon to hear this, but he was like, oh, okay. Well, how about we keep the shots close? And we're like, uh, no, let's go for it. So we went out and started engaging targets and shooting. Of course, all of us were pretty dialed in with the rifles and seven, eight, 900 yards was his brother's targets. We were hitting them over and over and over again. And he was just beside himself. She said, I never would have thought in a million years that a 223 could do that at seven, eight, 900 yards all day long. And all three of you, and we're pretty much all shooting the same load. We had a house load that we had forever. Um, just a really great training round. Um, yeah. He was like, it made him totally rethink the ability of a 223. What was more important was, is our abilities with the 223. We had no advantage in the wind whatsoever. We were shooting 75 grain bullets with a BC back then 0.435. We were running just over 2,900 feet per second. So not full max book, but in long barrels, of course. Um, so 26 inch barrels, getting everything out we can get and all three rifles whole way out to 900 yards all day long. No advantage in the wind, no advantage with, you know, ballistics. You can still see the hits on target. I mean, it was just hitting them hard enough that they would ring or you could see a move. No problem at all. And I will tell you that all three of us were better drivers with our match guns because of it. Mark went on to win, I believe, the senior division in PRS and then, of course, he's hosting and doing events north of Pittsburgh at their facility. But, you know, I would hope that if you ask Mark his thoughts on a 223 as a trainer and although some people would downplay the trainer just because of the cost and the expense and the work involved in it to get it up and running, I will tell you that, yeah. So, Joe, if you're listening to the podcast, which I hope you are, text me after you're done, after you listen to the whole thing or shoot me a call, I'd love to talk to you about it, but I would actually go in and learn to go slow to go fast. Use a caliber that's going to make you work harder and actually learn the art of shooting in the wind, not trying to beat the wind, right? I always say that Mother Nature isn't evil. I always say she's an equal opportunity destroyer. You know, she doesn't, not angry, she doesn't hate people, but she will certainly hand you your backside if you're not willing to just work with her and do what you need to do, right? So, everybody's put in the same predicament, everybody's shooting in the same conditions. I mean, some people might have a little stronger, a little less ones, but Mother Nature is what it is. She's always there and she never goes away. So, you can't really beat her into submission, just like you can't buy the biggest, safest sailboat or the biggest, safest, you know, whatever might be on the planet and you get in a big enough storm, you get in the wrong conditions, it blows you into the end of the rocks or it flips you over or get in some bad waves, whatever. You know, having that skill set is just as important as having a capable rifle, a capable motorcycle, a capable sailboat. Having the skills to operate them safely and being able to manage them at a level or control them at a level with finesse that most people wouldn't understand comes from using the smaller calibers, comes from using a trials bike, comes from using a very small sailboat to go out and get your butt whooped as you're sneaking out in this little person dinghy trying to master and learn the art of sailing. So, what calibers would I select? Let's wrap this up. I would absolutely select the .223. I think for most people one of the reasons I would go that route is just there's a great abundance of ammunition out there in a bulk gun that actually shoots really well and it's really inexpensive to buy. So, most people don't reload or a lot of people don't. This would allow you to build up a trend that you go out and, you know, buy a couple cases of ammunition and enjoy the rifle and not have to go bankrupt. And the same can be said and I would say even more accurate than the .223 would be doing a .22 arc. The .22 arc is based off the PPC cartridge. That cartridge is known to be one of the most accurate case designs ever made and that has set more world records than any other cartridge combined. So, I think the inherent accuracy is a little bit better or the chances of it and there's a really good quality ammunition on the market and it's still relatively inexpensive. Maybe not as cheap as a .223 but still less than $300 a case so you could buy two or three cases and enjoy the whole summer shooting it, enjoying it. And then lastly would be to reload. I would absolutely reload for both of these cartridges, single stage press, no problem at all, a couple hundred rounds at a time. It would make a world of difference in the accuracy, the repeatability, the standard deviation. Keep in mind, we're trying to read the wind and learn the art. We need good equipment. We just don't need it to beat the wind in the submission. So, we want the best accuracy we can get out of it. We want the best standard deviation. We want to remove as much of the rifle and the components as possible as far as causing some future errors or problems and that just all makes it fall back onto our shoulders as shooters. So, that's something that we're going to work on and knowing that when we see or make a mistake out there, it's not the rifle, it's not the load, it's not the ammunition, it's not the bullet, it's you. And I think that's a good place to be as far as learning the art as well. Lastly, would I buy factory or custom? Well, if you want a trainer, you know, I'm going to be honest here, I would absolutely buy a custom rifle. I would be looking for someone who's a master class and not just a world class receiver, maybe one that you can change the bolt head out on later to change it to a different caliber and make it your match gun. We'll talk about doing a switch barrel in the next podcast in which you can actually turn your match gun into a trainer if you would like and use it for that reason. But I would absolutely go custom all the way and I would absolutely do a top shelf barrel. I would absolutely be looking at a Krieger and I would build a specific trainer that you are going to go out and shoot lights out with. You're going to shoot bug holes at 100 yards. You know the rifle can shoot. You just now have to learn the wind and that really is where the key is. It's a hammer of a rifle and now you just got to learn to drive, right? And that wind's going to push you all over the place and hand you your backside. You're just going to keep trying and trying and trying and eventually you'll get more confident and more comfortable in making your calls and eventually you'll be like, oh man, this actually isn't all that bad. Now, I'm going to take one more podcast to talk about this particular subject and I'm going to leave this as a cliffhanger and we'll just throw it out there. What if I told you the mighty 338 and a 223 at a thousand yards on a 22 inch plate? 22 inch plate, 1,000 yards. The 338s bought you less than two mile per hour advantage in the wind before he misses the same target. You cannot beat the wind in submission. So if you want to hear the whole story, stop back over, check out the next podcast. She'll be released next week and we'll go into some details. All right, just some quick updates. We're going to wrap this one up. I really appreciate everybody hanging out and enjoying the podcast. I really encourage you to stop over, check out our sponsors and advertisers. They are products that we use, we are looking for and we always try to have three advertisers on our podcast. If you're interested, you can reach out to us if it's a product that we believe in, that we use, that's something that we can, with confidence, talk to our customers about. You can reach out to us here. It's contact at worldprecision.net. I also want to announce we just released and started making the 6GT. So we've gotten a lot of requests for it. It's been on the books here for a little while. We made our first Ace Chambers in it this week. Super excited to get all these put together. Really turned out great. Really interesting cartridge. I'm curious to see what you'll do and if it lives up to the hype, we're going to get out there and shoot it a little bit and see what it does. But the 6GT is now on the books for the Ace Chamber system and something that you can use to build your rifle. Also, we do make, of course, the .223 and the .22 Arc. Still one of my favorites and for the record, all of the school rifles next year are going to be .22 calibers. We're going to switch them over to probably the .22 Arc unless some advancements come in the .223. But I think we're going to end up, we have two of them on the line this year. I couldn't be happier with them with their ability for students to learn and grow and fundamentals, follow through, reading the wind, getting their feet underneath, some confidence over and over again. The .22 Arc has just shown that it's going to be a really great caliber and so we'll probably end up changing the last two school rifles over to it next year. So we're doing that here at the school for the same reason we just talked about and giving some advice to Joe. If you have anything you'd like to ask or you have any questions for us, please feel free to reach out to us at contact.wolfprecision.net and if you like this podcast and you'd like to share it, we really appreciate it as well. It helps us a lot in our standings. So we hope you enjoyed the content. Thank you so much for taking the time to join us. My name is Jamie Dotson. I'm your host and you're listening to Wolf Precisions, Long Range Shooting and Customer Rifle Building Podcast. Thank you.  
