## Item text
Hi and welcome to the Long Range Shooting and Custom Rifle Building Podcast presented by Wolf Precision Incorporated, where we learn about and share long range shooting and custom rifle building. I am your host, Jamie Dotson, and welcome to the show. Hi and welcome to episode 285 of Wolf Precision's Long Range Shooting and Custom Rifle Building Podcast. I am your host, Jamie Dotson, and welcome back to the show. In this episode, we're going to talk about the sticking point of wet mounting scope rings to your rifle. So it ought to be a great podcast, some food for thought. We just finished up a class. We've seen this. We did some research. And I have some things that I would like to talk about in this podcast for a lot of people go and adopt or look at or potentially bring this into their toolbox on how they mount their scope. So without further ado, here we go. So before we get started, I would like to say a quick thank you to MDT, maker of fine rifle chassis. They make a lot of different makes and models, as well as a lot of great accessories. I always say if you want to upgrade your rifle, one of the great ways to do it, whether it's factory or custom, is selecting a stock that fits you well and in long range fits you perfectly. So if you want to learn more about MDT, stop over to mdttac.com. That's mdtac.com. We'd also like to thank Krieger Barrows. They are part of our show. They are family owned and operated, cut rifled, legendary rifle barrels. One of the best in the world, in my opinion. They offer a new program called Krieger Direct where you can log in and order your barrel and have in as little as two to three business days from inventory. So if you want the best and you need it now, stop over to kriegerbarrels.com. That's kriegerbarrels.com today. Also want to throw out there that we are looking for a new podcast advertiser. If you are interested and it is parts and pieces that we use or a product that would actually be a benefit to our customers, please feel free to reach out to us. It's contact at wolfprecision.net. Contact at wolfprecision.net and put in the subject line podcast advertising. So we just finished up a long range shooting school. I am going to try to make this as efficient and effective as possible, but there's actually a lot to talk about. One of the things that we've seen in the class here and think of the podcast here about our way of sharing with you all kinds of tips and tricks to get you the best from your rifles, some food for thought on building it, maintaining it, and how you apply all of these parts and pieces. So we had a custom rifle come through the class. We didn't build it. And that custom rifle had a fair amount of small issues with it. It wasn't terrible. It was probably a minute and a half gun. We're not sure if it was part of the suppressor or the threading for the suppressor. We basically took care of the entire rifle and tore the whole thing down and put it all back together again during the class, trying to solve why this thing wasn't repeating, why it wasn't tracking. You know, we're almost at a point where we're going to end up having to have the customer use one of the school rifles. Now that said, it made it through the class. We just accepted the fact it was probably a minute and a half gun and ran with it. But one of the things that I seen when we pulled his scope off was the wet mounting that is recommended by Area 419. Now, before I go down this road, I want to say, hey, we've used Area 419 stuff in the past. They're not a bad company. They make good products. So this isn't a Area 419 subject. This is a subject with one of the things and processes that they're using. And I want people to know that this might not be the right choice. I want you to think about this for a little bit and then you make your decision in Q&As. So we pulled a scope off. And as soon as we pulled a scope off, we noticed that there was grease underneath the scope that mounted to the Picatinny rail, as well as on the Picatinny rail, as well as dig marks in the side of the Picatinny rail that was aluminum that was on his rifle. Now, one of the things that I thought about right away when I saw it was, A, why is there a bunch of lubrication underneath the contact point between your rings and the actual rail of the rifle? So you think about that flat surface that's coming down and we're drawing it down, trying to stick it to that surface and hold it in place and we want friction there. Why is that lubricated? And he went into, well, you know, Area 419 has this thing called wet mounting and they went through all these reasons why. And I did a bunch of research on it. And I just want to share with you food for thought because you might find a lot of people starting to do this and it's got some things that has to be played out yet. So I'm going to go over a couple of things when it comes to this. So obviously they were mounting to the rail at about 45 inch pounds, but I always remind people that 45 inch pounds is not the actual holding power. It's the actual torque that's being applied to the fasteners. And so we've got this thing called a sticky factor. And basically what it does is we're talking about when we're drawing that ring down onto the Picatinny rail, we want there to be not just torque pinching that and holding it down. But when we do that, we want those surfaces to have some friction with each other. And just from running the math and the numbers here, when you wet lube or you put lubrication down underneath that ring, you can be losing 50 to 75% of your friction. Now, I know one of the things that they're saying is mount the scope rings, which is a one piece unit so that the bar or crosshatch unit actually sets up against and tight against the front part of the Picatinny rail. So in other words, they've created almost like a recoil lug on where they want you to put that and in effect, eliminating that holding power or the friction that actually is trying to force the scope to move back and forth. One of the things I want to caution you with that is that we have found the best repeatability and I know I might get hung out here, but I can only explain to you from experience of I have probably taken more scopes off and re-zeroed a rifle than almost anyone that I know. And probably most of the people in this industry that are selling this stuff simply because we have been running a shooting school for 20 years and every class we take the scopes off to clean the rifles and we put them back on and they always repeat. So I have done this thousands of times, not 10 times, not 50 times, not a chat GBT, or AI, whatever you want to research and development, but I have literally done this hands-on thousands of times. And the best way I found to not just have the best repeatability, especially using something like badger rings and so on and so forth, is to make sure you use torque wrenches, which is something that this gentleman did not do, right? And we'll talk about action screws in the next podcast, but it seemed like they were over-tightened a little bit, but you could also see marks and I'm not sure if that's from him over-tightening it or it trying to move, right? But make sure that in my mind, I don't want that bar that hangs down in the pick rail touching fore or aft. We actually, and I know this sounds counterproductive, but I want you to follow me on this one. We actually find that even with the big magnums, we floated in the middle so that it is not making contact with the pick rail front or back. And there's multiple reasons for this. Number one, when we're clamping down, we have a C-clamp pulling down and drawing that ring onto the actual pick rail. One of the things that I think puts a lot of stress on a scope is the fact that all of these things bend and twist, and we'll talk about yaw and roll here in just a moment as you're shooting. So it's not just recoil coming back, but the actual action and the rings and bases flex a little bit, which translate that flex to your scope. If we mount that solid piece that's on the bottom of your rings against the pick rail fore or aft, what we've done is we've made a pendulum to where as the pick rail flexes, it actually has something to push against, putting pressure against the entire scope ring top to bottom and actually trying to push it further, if that makes sense. So what I try to do in my thought was let's let it float in the middle. We have the friction holding it from going back and forth, which is 55, 65 inch pounds in some of the cases of the rings that you use. It's not going to allow it to move, but it does allow it to sort of absorb that roll and yaw and twist without having something that hangs down much, much further than the top of the rings that it can actually push on or pull against and start putting additional torque on your scope. So we float that for that reason that the rings are going to clamped. They're not going to go anywhere. They're holding both on the C clamps on the side that are digging as well as the pull down force and the friction. And it's never losing all that friction, by the way, or the contact points, but it allows it to sort of float. If it bends both fore and aft that it doesn't have that contact piece that hangs deep low into the pick rail, giving it a leverage point to sort of push against now really allowing that scope rather than floating on top to actually start pushing and pulling and bending and, and actually putting a lot of wear and tear on your scope. For me, when you're talking about wet mounting, and I would agree that there is some repeatability that happens. And there is some holding strength that happens when you actually loop threads compared to torquing something without loops on them. There's a lot of math involved in this. However, though, the one thing that I don't like when you're talking about wet mounting a scope is we're depending on that recoil lug of the front part of the bottom of the scope mount pushing forward against the pick rail to hold it in place, basically absorbing the recoil. But that bar does not protect against what's more important, I think, in over big picture is roll and yaw. So you can get a twist. The vibrations don't just kick backwards. That harmonics and everything bending and torquing is both twisting up and down and rolling a little bit and sort of putting some lateral torque on stuff. And so you have both not just recoil force against the back there, but you actually have roll, twist, and yaw to deal with, in which case that lubrication is going to remove all the clamping friction force that we have and possibly allow that thing to move. There has to be a lot of science in this. So in other words, I'm not going to say that they're right or they're wrong, but I'm going to caution you because there's a great sales pitch for this. And there's a lot of things that they're talking about. So how do we test this and how do we know for sure? Well, first of all, one of the things that I look at when I look at stuff like this is when the company itself, and again, this isn't anything with Area 419. This is not an Area 419 issue. I have no action going on with them. This is me talking to my customer saying, all right, let's listen to both sides of the story here. When we are opening the rings up to bare aluminum, so that way we get more gripping power on the scope so the scope can't move. And then we lubricate where the rings actually contact the scope base. That's sort of like two different things being said for both things that are important that we want to keep from moving and sliding around. In one case, we're not just using anodization, but we're actually using bare aluminum because it's more sticky, right? And when we're holding our scope, but then we're reducing friction as we tighten up our scope onto our base. And we're adding a bunch of lubricant underneath there. And this customer sort of overdid it a little bit. And by the way, we enjoyed having him here at the school. We really liked everything that he did here. It wasn't an issue with the customer to be here. We took a lot of extra time in the class to try to go through his rifle piece by piece and get it working. But the fact that that lubrication, one of the things that we're depending on when we actually put that scope ring down onto the base is we're actually depending on the friction. Once we clamp those two together, those surfaces being able to have a sticky factor to them while the scope is going through recoil as well as roll, yaw, and twist. In which case, now we've made a surface that can actually slip and move around. And we don't want that scope to move 0.0001. We don't want it to move at all. And so I'm just going to share with you my personal experiences with dry mounting and using high quality rings. And, you know, Area 419, of course, says that you can dry mount or wet mount, I believe, on their instructions. And so it's not like this is the only way you can install their scope either. I'm just giving you food for thought because, again, this was being brought up at our school and when I popped the scope off. I was really surprised to see all the lubricant underneath it. So to wrap this up and to talk a little bit more in depth about it, if in my mind, if maximum friction is what our desirable outcome is when we're actually mounting our scope on our rings and think about the anodization being moved to raw aluminum or gripping our scope, I would say the maximum friction should hold true as something that we're desirable or that I would want when we're mounting our rings to our base. So the questions I think that we need to explore is maybe wet mounting gives more consistency of seating during installation. Think of like drawing our scope down and we've got everything lubed up that, you know, nothing can bind. But dry mounting might actually give you more resistance to microscopic movements after installation. And so for a precision rifle, I would argue that we want maximum grip on the scope, but we want lubrication under the rings. And I think that deserves a closer look and maybe a little bit more research. So I will tell you that we had a hard time keeping this rifle zeroed. We had a hard time keeping it same point of impact. I did go over the rifle personally from front to back, trying to make sure everything was the way it was supposed to be and tighten them to spec. So to wrap this up one more time, I will say that, you know, I can't claim that wet mounting is wrong and there's a lot of testing that would have to be done with it. And again, it may improve some seating consistencies, but it's equally reasonable to ask whether reducing friction at a critical precision interface between the two particular parts, especially for a heavy recoil rifle, that you wouldn't want that friction there as well. And, you know, putting that lubrication there. So the bottom line is torque creates a clamping force. Fiction creates a sticky factor. And once we put oil on between those two surfaces, you haven't changed the fact that we've tightened it down to 45 inch pounds, but you have changed how much the clamping force can resist movement. So that lubrication underneath it. So there's a lot to be talked about. There's a lot more we can dive into this. We're going to do some testing here. Of course, it was first time that I've really seen it that applied to a rifle. And I'm not saying ARRI 419 is right or wrong. But I will tell you that if you see this being advertised on the internet, I mean, it's the latest, greatest craze. I would pause and I might rethink doing this unless somebody comes along and shows the benefits or proves that nothing is moving. Again, you know, we can only draw from our experiences over 20 plus years of taking hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of scopes on and off again with the ring still attached to them and having great success with repeatability. Just a real quick update. We do have our practical tactical shooters challenge that is in November. That is for active match shooters. So those that are actually actively participating in long range shooting matches that know the rifle, know their dope. We are 100% suppressed and you can run a suppressor for the event if you need it. But again, this event is suppressed. We have people signing up from as far away as down south. We're really looking forward to holding this event. We haven't done one in a couple of years. If you're interested, it looked like our link went down on our actual page. So if you go to our wolfprecision.net page and you go to practical tactical at the bottom of that page, there is a link that will send you over to our registration page where you can sign up and register and take care of it and plan on attending. And if you go to our store, you will see that under long range shooting and matches, you'll see it listed there. You can click on there and register there as well. But it looked like the link went down for whatever reason. We don't know. We actually fixed it for those that might've been trying to sign up. I am more than welcome to stop back over. We also announced our 2027 shooting school dates with our first class in Florida that we've been holding in about three or four years. So if you'd like to join us in Florida at the end of February, we'll be down in Florida teaching at Immokalee. That registration is open. We have students signing up now. It's a great place to come down and enjoy some winter shooting and have some great time with the rifle. At that event, suppressors are not mandatory and there are not school rifles available. This is a travel class and we do not take rifles to the travel classes. And then also don't forget our 2027 shooting school season is open. We are starting to take registrations and classes are starting to fill and keep in mind in our at home classes. We are 100% suppressed when they allow five students in a class at the Immokalee event. I believe we're going to allow as many as eight to 10, just because it's a remote class and that facility is so much bigger and we can handle it. So I'm looking forward to having everybody attend the classes. Also, don't forget, we have our online long-range shooting school and we have reloading content going up all this winter as we get into our Wolfpack program, which includes our online long-range shooting school. It includes our rifle maintenance and carry series, which we'll be diving into a lot of the scope, rings, and bases stuff here. And then it also includes our online reloading class. And we're actually filming more episodes that will be posted up later this week. So you can learn more about the Wolfpack. It's $49 a month. You can sign up for the year or for the month and lots of great content. Years and years of filling it. We have probably close to 25 hours to 30 hours of content with more stuff going up regularly and two and a half to three years of filming it. So it's everything that we've learned about trying to share with you to get the most from your rifle, whether it's factory or custom. So there's a lot that you can learn from all the tips and tricks that we teach through the classes. So sign up for the Wolfpack at wolfprecision.net. Well, I'd like to thank everybody for taking the time to join us. I really appreciate it. If you enjoyed this podcast, please feel free to share it. It helps us a lot in our ratings. If you'd like to become a sponsor or an advertiser and you have a product that's worthwhile, our customers taking a look at or our listeners, we want to check it out. We'll make sure it's a good fit for us in our business. But we do have one slot open for an advertiser. You can contact us at wolfprecision.net. Contact at wolfprecision.net. If you would like to talk about a product or service that you would offer, that would be a good fit for both our customers, our listeners, and for Wolf Precision as a team. So thank you everybody for taking the time to join us. My name is Jamie Dotson. I'm your host and you're listening to Wolf Precision's long range shooting and customer rifle building podcast.
